Agent: Yes — from our notes you decided to grow marigolds. You’re a beginner home gardener and chose marigold as your new plant.

Quick care recap and next steps:
- Light: Full sun (6+ hours/day) — they bloom best in bright light.  
- Soil: Well-draining, average garden soil; they’re not fussy.  
- Water: Moderate — water when the top inch of soil is dry. Avoid waterlogging.  
- Planting: Start seeds indoors 4–6 weeks before last frost or sow directly after frost passes. Space plants 6–12 inches apart depending on variety.  
- Maintenance: Deadhead spent flowers to extend blooming; light feeding once a month if growth seems slow.  
- Pests/benefits: Relatively pest-resistant; marigolds can help deter some garden pests and are good companion plants for vegetables.  
- Timing: Continuous blooms through summer into fall if kept deadheaded and in sun.

